For every fixed pair of real numbers $D,r\ge1$, there is $S_0(D,r)$ such that the following holds for $S\ge S_0(D,r)$. Suppose that $X,Y$ are real numbers and $N$ is a nonnegative integer satisfying $$\frac{S}{2\log S}\le \log X\le DS,\qquad
 X^{1/2}\le Y\le X,\qquad N\le e^{DS}.$$ Then $$\label{eq:divisor-moment}
 \sum_{1\le h\le Y}d_X(N+h)^r\le Y\exp(S^{1/4}).$$

For the deterministic list $T_0$ constructed above, every level $0\le j<d$ with $X=X_j>e^S$, and every integer $1\le\ell\le H:=\lfloor e^{4\eta m}\rfloor$, one has $$\label{eq:deterministic-mean}
 \frac1X\sum_{X_{j+1}<u\le X}
 \left|\frac1{|T_0|}\sum_{t\in T_0}\mathrm e(\ell Ct/u)\right|^2
 \le e^{-0.01m}$$ for all sufficiently large $S$, uniformly in $C$.
